WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.318
This is Make Your First Beats, by Monobox. Drum machines and rhythm, one step at a time.

00:00:07.118 --> 00:00:10.079
Today: shaping the sound and from pattern to song.

00:00:11.879 --> 00:00:16.231
Shaping the sound. Tune, length, layers and balance.

00:00:17.031 --> 00:00:31.442
The rhythm is half of a beat; the sounds are the other half. The same pattern sounds completely different with a short, punchy kick or a long, booming one. Most drum machines give you a few controls for each sound. These are the most common.

00:00:32.942 --> 00:00:33.469
Tune.

00:00:34.269 --> 00:00:55.618
Tune or pitch raises or lowers the sound. A lower snare sounds fatter and heavier; a higher one sounds tighter and snappier. With long kicks, like the classic 808 kick, the tuning decides which note the kick plays. If the track has a bass line or chords, tune the kick so that it fits the key, or it can sound out of tune with the music.

00:00:57.118 --> 00:00:57.690
Decay.

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:12.063
Decay or length sets how long the sound rings. A short kick leaves space for a bass line; a long one can be the bass line itself, as in a lot of trap. Short hats sound tight; longer ones sound open and loose.

00:01:13.563 --> 00:01:14.218
Layers.

00:01:15.018 --> 00:01:31.725
Two sounds played on the same step make a new sound. A clap layered on a snare adds width; a short, clicky kick layered on a deep one adds punch. When you layer, make the two sounds complement each other: one for the body, one for the attack, not two that do the same thing.

00:01:33.225 --> 00:01:33.896
Balance.

00:01:34.696 --> 00:01:52.424
The level of each sound decides what the listener notices first. A common starting point: kick and snare loudest, hats clearly lower, percussion lower still. Pan places sounds left or right. Keep the kick and snare in the centre, and spread hats and percussion a little to the sides to make room.

00:01:53.924 --> 00:01:54.624
Effects.

00:01:55.424 --> 00:01:57.716
Many drum machines have built-in effects:

00:01:58.516 --> 00:02:04.677
Reverb puts a sound in a space. A little on the snare adds depth; a lot pushes it far away.

00:02:05.477 --> 00:02:10.592
Delay repeats a sound in time. It is good on percussion, and rarely good on the kick.

00:02:11.392 --> 00:02:17.480
Filter removes highs or lows. Sweeping a filter on the whole beat is a classic way to build tension.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:21.570
Distortion or drive adds grit and makes sounds feel louder.

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:30.026
Tip: Use effects sparingly on the kick. Reverb and delay on a kick tend to blur the low end and make the whole beat sound muddy.

00:02:31.526 --> 00:02:32.267
Practice.

00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:39.286
First: take your favourite beat from the ebook. Tune the kick up and down and listen to how the mood changes.

00:02:40.086 --> 00:02:43.514
Second: make the kick very short, then very long.

00:02:44.314 --> 00:02:49.544
Third: layer a clap on the snare, and adjust the levels until they sound like one sound.

00:02:50.344 --> 00:02:54.954
Fourth: put a little reverb on the snare only, then compare with and without.

00:02:56.754 --> 00:03:00.799
From pattern to song. Variations, fills and arrangement.

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:11.629
A great loop is a start, not a song. A track needs change: parts that build, drop and return. With a drum machine you build that change from a few patterns.

00:03:13.129 --> 00:03:14.708
Make a family of patterns.

00:03:15.508 --> 00:03:21.524
Start from your main beat and make variations of it. Each one copies the main pattern and changes a little:

00:03:22.324 --> 00:03:24.582
Main: the beat that carries the track.

00:03:25.382 --> 00:03:32.742
Light: fewer elements, for example only kick and hats, or only hats and percussion. Good for intros and breaks.

00:03:33.542 --> 00:03:40.529
Full: the main beat plus extra percussion, open hats or a busier kick. Good for the most intense parts.

00:03:41.329 --> 00:03:46.013
Fill: a one-bar pattern with a snare or tom roll that leads into the next section.

00:03:47.513 --> 00:03:48.189
Fills.

00:03:48.989 --> 00:04:00.651
A fill tells the listener that something is about to change. It usually comes in the last bar, or the last beat, before a new section. A simple one: snare hits on every step of the last beat.

00:04:01.451 --> 00:04:03.505
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:04:05.005 --> 00:04:06.221
Sections and length.

00:04:07.021 --> 00:04:21.515
Dance music is almost always built in phrases of 8 bars, so its sections usually last 8, 16 or 32 bars. Changes tend to happen at the start of those blocks, so listeners feel them coming. A simple structure for a beat:

00:04:22.315 --> 00:04:25.416
First: intro: 8 bars of the light pattern.

00:04:26.216 --> 00:04:32.331
Second: main: 16 bars of the main pattern, with a fill in bar 8 and bar 16.

00:04:33.131 --> 00:04:36.289
Third: break: 8 bars with the kick removed.

00:04:37.089 --> 00:04:40.794
Fourth: main again: 16 bars of the full pattern.

00:04:41.594 --> 00:04:44.791
Fifth: outro: 8 bars of the light pattern.

00:04:46.291 --> 00:04:47.401
Chaining patterns.

00:04:48.201 --> 00:05:01.254
Most drum machines let you chain patterns into a song, or switch between them live. Switching by hand while the beat plays is a good way to find a structure: play around until it feels right, then write it down or save the chain.

00:05:02.754 --> 00:05:04.665
Removing is as strong as adding.

00:05:05.465 --> 00:05:15.204
Taking a sound away is one of the most powerful moves in an arrangement. When the kick drops out for four bars and then comes back, it hits harder than if it had played all along.

00:05:16.004 --> 00:05:24.782
Tip: Before you finish a track, listen to it from start to end without stopping, and note where your attention drifts. That is where it needs a change.

00:05:26.282 --> 00:05:27.023
Practice.

00:05:27.823 --> 00:05:32.707
First: make four patterns from one beat: main, light, full and fill.

00:05:33.507 --> 00:05:36.635
Second: chain them into the five-part structure above.

00:05:37.435 --> 00:05:44.259
Third: play it back from the start. Mark one place where removing a sound would make the return stronger, and try it.

00:05:46.059 --> 00:05:47.151
That's all for today.

00:05:47.951 --> 00:05:49.176
If you want to keep going:

00:05:49.976 --> 00:05:58.911
Monobox is the app behind the ebook. It is a drum machine and groovebox: a place to make beats, step by step, and turn them into grooves.

00:05:59.711 --> 00:06:02.553
Everything the ebook teaches is meant to be tried with it:

00:06:03.353 --> 00:06:05.335
programming patterns on a step grid.

00:06:06.135 --> 00:06:08.267
knowing what each drum sound does.

00:06:09.067 --> 00:06:11.377
building the classic rhythms of each genre.

00:06:12.177 --> 00:06:15.067
adding swing, dynamics and sound design.

00:06:15.867 --> 00:06:17.800
turning a pattern into a whole track.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:23.131
This ebook is free. Share it with anyone who wants to make their first beats.

00:06:23.931 --> 00:06:27.905
This episode of Make Your First Beats was narrated with a synthetic voice.

